{
  "meta": {
    "library": "Seneca — On Providence (Stewart translation)",
    "notes": [
      "Stewart translation (1889), US public domain; Standard Ebooks text (CC0). Footnote numbers removed.",
      "Skipped on purpose: everything about Cato, and chapter 6's passages on dying (suicide-adjacent); those never go on the channels.",
      "Chapter 6 lines credited '(God speaking)' are Seneca's imagined speech of God to good men."
    ]
  },
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-01",
      "type": "passage_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 1",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "growth",
        "purpose"
      ],
      "text": "When therefore you see men who are good and acceptable to the gods toiling, sweating, painfully struggling upwards, while bad men run riot and are steeped in pleasures, reflect that modesty pleases us in our sons, and forwardness in our house-born slave-boys; that the former are held in check by a somewhat stern rule, whereas the boldness of the latter is encouraged. Be thou sure that God acts in like manner: He does not pet the good man: He tries him, hardens him, and fits him for Himself.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 41,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 1"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-02",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 1",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "growth"
      ],
      "text": "Be thou sure that God acts in like manner: He does not pet the good man: He tries him, hardens him, and fits him for Himself.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 14,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 1"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-03",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 2",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "perspective",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "It does not matter what you bear, but how you bear it.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 8,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-04",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 2",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "resilience",
        "adversity",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "Unbroken prosperity cannot bear a single blow; but he who has waged an unceasing strife with his misfortunes has gained a thicker skin by his sufferings, yields to no disaster, and even though he fall yet fights on his knee.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 20,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-05",
      "type": "passage_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 2",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "training",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "We see athletes, who study only their bodily strength, engage in contests with the strongest of men, and insist that those who train them for the arena should put out their whole strength when practising with them: they endure blows and maltreatment, and, if they cannot find any single person who is their match, they engage with several at once: their strength and courage droop without an antagonist: they can only prove how great and how mighty it is by proving how much they can endure.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 40,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-06",
      "type": "passage_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 2",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "inner life",
        "resilience"
      ],
      "text": "Just as so many rivers, so many showers of rain from the clouds, such a number of medicinal springs, do not alter the taste of the sea, indeed, do not so much as soften it, so the pressure of adversity does not affect the mind of a brave man; for the mind of a brave man maintains its balance and throws its own complexion over all that takes place, because it is more powerful than any external circumstances.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 37,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-07",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 2",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "comfort",
        "weakness"
      ],
      "text": "Those who are surfeited with ease break down not only with labour, but with mere motion and by their own weight.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 12,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-08",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 2",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "courage",
        "challenge"
      ],
      "text": "Behold a sight worthy to be viewed by a god interested in his own work, behold a pair worthy of a god, a brave man matched with evil fortune, especially if he himself has given the challenge.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 19,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-09",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 3",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca, quoting Demetrius",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "growth",
        "fortune"
      ],
      "text": "“No one,” said he, “seems to me more unhappy than the man whom no misfortune has ever befallen.”",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 11,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 3 (quoting Demetrius)"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-10",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 3",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "challenge",
        "glory",
        "courage"
      ],
      "text": "A gladiator deems it a disgrace to be matched with an inferior, and knows that to win without danger is to win without glory.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 3"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-11",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 3",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "fortune",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "Just so doth Fortune; she seeks out the bravest to match herself with, passes over some with disdain, and makes for the most unyielding and upright of men, to exert her strength against them.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 18,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 3"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-12",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "character",
        "self-knowledge"
      ],
      "text": "You are a great man; but how am I to know it, if fortune gives you no opportunity of showing your virtue?",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-13",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "self-knowledge",
        "testing"
      ],
      "text": "…a man cannot know himself without a trial; no one ever learnt what he could do without putting himself to the test…",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-14",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "virtue",
        "fear"
      ],
      "text": "Do not, I beg you, dread those things which the immortal gods apply to our minds like spurs: misfortune is virtue’s opportunity.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-15",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "preparation"
      ],
      "text": "Misfortunes press hardest on those who are unacquainted with them: the yoke feels heavy to the tender neck.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 11,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-16",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "fear",
        "experience",
        "courage"
      ],
      "text": "The recruit turns pale at the thought of a wound: the veteran, who knows that he has often won the victory after losing blood, looks boldly at his own flowing gore.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 16,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-17",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "character"
      ],
      "text": "…you can judge of a pilot in a storm, of a soldier in a battle.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 10,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-18",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "comfort",
        "mind",
        "truth"
      ],
      "text": "While all excesses are hurtful, excess of comfort is the most hurtful of all; it affects the brain; it leads men’s minds into vain imaginings; it spreads a thick cloud over the boundaries of truth and falsehood.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 19,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-19",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "comfort",
        "adversity",
        "virtue"
      ],
      "text": "Is it not better, with virtue by one’s side, to endure continual misfortune, than to burst with an endless surfeit of good things?",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-20",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "growth",
        "courage"
      ],
      "text": "Fortune lashes and mangles us: well, let us endure it: it is not cruelty, it is a struggle, in which the oftener we engage the braver we shall become.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 16,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-21",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "training",
        "fortune",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "The strongest part of the body is that which is exercised by the most frequent use: we must entrust ourselves to Fortune to be hardened by her against herself: by degrees she will make us a match for herself.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 20,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-22",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "fear",
        "practice"
      ],
      "text": "Familiarity with danger leads us to despise it.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 6,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-23",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "endurance",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "…that part of each man which he exercises is the strongest: so by endurance the mind becomes able to despise the power of misfortunes.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-24",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "growth",
        "resilience"
      ],
      "text": "No tree which the wind does not often blow against is firm and strong; for it is stiffened by the very act of being shaken, and plants its roots more securely.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 16,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-25",
      "type": "passage_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 4",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "growth",
        "comfort"
      ],
      "text": "No tree which the wind does not often blow against is firm and strong; for it is stiffened by the very act of being shaken, and plants its roots more securely: those which grow in a sheltered valley are brittle: and so it is to the advantage of good men, and causes them to be undismayed, that they should live much amidst alarms, and learn to bear with patience what is not evil save to him who endures it ill.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 38,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 4"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-26",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "Fire tries gold, misfortune tries brave men.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 6,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-27",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "courage",
        "risk"
      ],
      "text": "It is the part of grovellers and cowards to follow the safe track; courage loves a lofty path.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 11,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-28",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "virtue",
        "difficulty"
      ],
      "text": "See how high virtue has to climb: you may be sure that it has no safe path to tread.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 11,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-29",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "acceptance",
        "will",
        "freedom"
      ],
      "text": "I am constrained to nothing, I suffer nothing against my will, nor am I God’s slave, but his willing follower, and so much the more because I know that everything is ordained and proceeds according to a law that endures forever.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 21,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-30",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "acceptance",
        "adversity"
      ],
      "text": "Wherefore, everything must be patiently endured, because events do not fall in our way, as we imagine, but come by a regular law.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 13,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-31",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "acceptance",
        "loss",
        "contentment"
      ],
      "text": "…let us be cheerful whatever befalls, and stoutly reflect that it is not anything of our own that perishes.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 11,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-32",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 5",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "persistence"
      ],
      "text": "…he must make his way in spite of fortune: he will meet with much that is hard which he must soften, much that is rough that he must make smooth.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 16,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 5"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-33",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 6",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca, God speaking, in Seneca's dialogue",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "self-reliance",
        "contentment",
        "inner life"
      ],
      "text": "I have placed every good thing within your own breasts: it is your good fortune not to need any good fortune.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 12,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 6 (God speaking)"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-34",
      "type": "passage_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 6",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca, God speaking, in Seneca's dialogue",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "wealth",
        "status",
        "truth"
      ],
      "text": "Those men whom you regard as fortunate, if you could see, not their outward show, but their hidden life, are really unhappy, mean, and base, ornamented on the outside like the walls of their houses: that good fortune of theirs is not sound and genuine: it is only a veneer, and that a thin one.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 27,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 6 (God speaking)"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-35",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 6",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca, God speaking, in Seneca's dialogue",
      "excerpt": false,
      "themes": [
        "adversity",
        "strength"
      ],
      "text": "Well, as I have not been able to remove these from your path, I have given your minds strength to combat all: bear them bravely.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
        "status": "pending_check_translation_match"
      },
      "est_seconds": 14,
      "display_credit": "Seneca, On Providence 6 (God speaking)"
    },
    {
      "id": "SD-PR-36",
      "type": "quote_reel",
      "author": "Seneca",
      "work": "On Providence",
      "ref": "On Providence 6",
      "translator": "Aubrey Stewart (1889)",
      "attribution": "Seneca, God speaking, in Seneca's dialogue",
      "excerpt": true,
      "themes": [
        "fortune",
        "inner life"
      ],
      "text": "…despise fortune; I have given her no weapon that can reach the mind.",
      "verified": true,
      "source_url": "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/seneca/dialogues/aubrey-stewart/text/on-providence",
      "audio": {
        "source": "LibriVox",
